 "text": "When generative AI first came about, it only processed text, but with newer multimodal models like GBT5.2 that's now in co-pilot, it can work with far more than text alone. And one of those modalities is artifacts, which is a catch all term for photos, screenshots, documents, and more. Uploading artifacts into Copilot is straightforward and the process is largely the same across platforms So let's take a look at how we can upload an artifact to Copilot and how Copilot works with it. So here I have a handwritten note with indicators for a AI fluency matrix we developed, and I want CodePilot to help me generate some more indicator ideas. So I open CodePilot on my phone and I tap the image or plus icon. And here I have a few upload options. So I can upload photos and files from my phone or use the camera to take a photo directly. On a desktop, I can also upload the image file by clicking on plus and then adding images or files. On work copilot, I can click on plus, then upload images or files or even add work content, after which I can select files from, for example, OneDrive or SharePoint. And in both of these versions you can also paste an image from your clipboard or just drag and drop it into the prom box. And then depending on the app and platform you may also be able to share an image to co pilot from other apps like Photos. But for this demo I'm going to choose camera and then take a photo of the page. And if you already have a photo or another artifact saved on your computer or phone you can upload it directly. Before asking Copilot to suggest anything I first give it a brief introduction of what this photo is about and clearly state what I want Copilot to do. So I'm saying here, this is a brainstorming note on the indicators for AI fluency levels. First tell me what you can clearly see and then mark any unclear text as illegible and do not organize or improve anything yet. So this prompt is really important because it helps ensure that Copilot extracts only what's written and reduces confident mistakes, which happen especially with messy handwriting, blurry photos or images containing names, numbers and dates. But as you can see, Copilot can read my handwriting pretty well. And so once I have verified that Copilot transcribed my words correctly, I can then keep working on these ideas. So for example, now I can ask it something like, arrange this by level and then suggest three indicators for each level. If I want Copilot's answer to be even more relevant, I can also attach additional supporting files or images and then ask Copilot to consider them together in the same prompt. And that's really how you can save time and reduce the translation text. Here are a few important things to know about uploading files in Copilot. Copilot supports common image and document formats including photos, PDFs and Microsoft Office files. Image understanding works with stand alone images and with images inside documents like screenshots or scanned pages in a PDF. Limits vary by account and plan. What you can upload may also depend on your organization's security settings. Before you share anything sensitive or confidential with Copilot, always check that you're allowed to upload it.",
